1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,160
Claude Ship: you think 1 PR must cost you 4 stops,

2
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:06,935
but AI actually tries the fixes and approves it,

3
00:00:06,975 --> 00:00:08,395
if your policy allows.

4
00:00:08,895 --> 00:00:09,615
Here's how.

5
00:00:09,915 --> 00:00:12,326
Before, an AI review only left comments.

6
00:00:12,626 --> 00:00:14,790
You stopped your next task for a failing check,

7
00:00:14,830 --> 00:00:15,930
a merge conflict,

8
00:00:15,970 --> 00:00:18,447
a comment, then chased someone to approve.

9
00:00:18,947 --> 00:00:23,015
Now, in beta, PR Steward works on all three while you keep coding,

10
00:00:23,055 --> 00:00:24,483
and notes what it changed.

11
00:00:24,983 --> 00:00:27,715
Then Claude Approvals reads your team's policy file,

12
00:00:27,755 --> 00:00:29,050
like a harbor rulebook:

13
00:00:29,090 --> 00:00:31,680
small routine boats it can stamp itself;

14
00:00:31,720 --> 00:00:33,432
risky ones can need people.

15
00:00:33,932 --> 00:00:35,660
Anthropic's Claude Code team,

16
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:37,730
with Claude's review as one layer,

17
00:00:37,770 --> 00:00:40,120
more than doubled merged pull requests,

18
00:00:40,160 --> 00:00:41,332
June to September.

19
00:00:41,832 --> 00:00:42,870
So, what we learned:

20
00:00:42,910 --> 00:00:44,049
in our example,

21
00:00:44,089 --> 00:00:46,175
1 pull request stopped you 4 times;

22
00:00:46,215 --> 00:00:49,440
now 0, if the fixes work and your policy allows.

23
00:00:49,940 --> 00:00:50,643
Want more?

24
00:00:50,943 --> 00:00:53,473
Subscribe, and comment what we should explain next.
